// nmap's own comments and its own handful of documented non-IEEE additions -- see the
// fetched file's own header) and re-emits them as this project's own lookup table, the
// same "facts extracted, re-expressed in this codebase's own structures" sourcing
// convention already used for e.g. stp.hpp/ffhse.hpp (see their own file comments).
//
// Three tables, one per block size, each sorted ascending by its N-bit prefix (stored
// right-justified in a uint64_t) for binary search. oui_vendor_lookup (resolver.cpp)
// tries MA-S (36-bit, most specific) first, then MA-M (28-bit), then MA-L (24-bit) --
// see its own comment for why that order is correct (MA-M/MA-S are sub-delegations
// within specific MA-L blocks IEEE reserves for exactly this purpose, so a MAC can
// legitimately match more than one table at once, and the most specific one is always
// the right answer).
